    "detail": "Only this SGLang server (and, for the speed and long-context rows, the small cache-busting proxy in front of it) was resident; the image server that normally runs on the box was stopped, and nvidia-smi listed no other compute process. The only other running container was a small host metrics exporter (python, about 10 MB, no GPU).",
    "isolation_check": "nvidia-smi compute apps -> 1532664, .venv/bin/python 1532720, sglang::scheduler ; docker ps -> 1 containers; load1 at start 0.92"
  },
  "gotchas": [
    {
      "severity": "info",
      "text": "Tool calling needs SGLang with the minicpm5 tool-call parser (sgl-project/sglang#25600). The openbmb cookbook says to install SGLang from main because v0.5.12.post1 lacked it; the PyPI 0.5.20 wheel used here ships it, so no source build is needed any more. The model writes XML tool calls (<function name=\"x\"><param name=\"k\">v</param></function>) and they come back as OpenAI tool_calls with finish_reason tool_calls, with thinking on and off."
    },
    {
      "severity": "info",
      "text": "SGLang has no MiniCPM5 reasoning parser, but the model uses plain <think> ... </think>, so --reasoning-parser qwen3 splits it into reasoning_content. With enable_thinking left unset the template adds no <think> prefix; the model opens the tag itself and the qwen3 parser still catches it."
    },
    {
      "severity": "info",
      "text": "flashinfer JIT-compiles its prefill kernels on the first request and shells out to ninja. In a venv that is not activated the first prefill dies with FileNotFoundError: ninja and the scheduler exits; put the venv bin directory on PATH before starting the server."
    },
    {
      "severity": "info",
      "text": "In a llama.cpp deployment the card warns that the default min_p 0.05 causes repetition and asks for min_p 0; the SGLang default min_p is already 0."
    },
    {
      "severity": "info",
      "text": "SGLang RadixAttention reuses any cached prefix and has no per-request switch to skip it, and the prefill, long-context and serving workloads reuse the same prompts and haystacks. To keep prefill cold without turning the cache off for the whole server, the speed and long-context rows went through a small proxy on the box that prepends a unique HTML comment (<!-- 12 hex chars -->, about 12 tokens) to the first message of every request, so the shared prefix is only the few chat-template tokens before it. Input token counts include the comment."
    },
    {
      "severity": "info",
      "text": "DSpark does not start on this box with SGLang 0.5.20 at the default decode CUDA-graph size. DSpark verifies block_size+1 = 8 tokens per sequence, so capturing the decode graph at its default max batch of 256 asks flashinfer for a 3,137,536-byte prefill workspace out of a fixed 1,048,576-byte AlignedAllocator and capture fails with a buffer overflow, taking the scheduler with it. 0.5.20 exposes no workspace-size flag, so the only knob is --cuda-graph-max-bs-decode; it is set to 64 here and recorded in the args. That leaves admission alone - max_running_requests is still 512 and still confirmed from /get_server_info - but batches above 64 run without a captured decode graph, so the concurrency points above 64 in this config are NOT arg-identical to the plain bf16 2B rows and the two should not be diffed at those points. The single-user and low-concurrency rows are the comparable ones."
    },
    {
      "severity": "info",
      "text": "The DSpark drafter costs KV pool: max_total_num_tokens falls from 2,034,852 on the plain 2B to 1,798,013 here at the same --mem-fraction-static 0.75, and the drafter itself loads as a separate 0.93 GB Qwen3DSparkModel."
    }
  ],
